WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.236
(ominous music)

00:00:02.236 --> 00:00:05.069
(dramatic music)

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.271
- [Rick] Here we go!
(dog panting)

00:00:08.271 --> 00:00:09.730
- Eh?

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.152
- [Reporter] Who are these
unstoppable warriors?

00:00:12.152 --> 00:00:13.280
- [Katie] We're the Mitchells,

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.597
the only people who can save the world.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:18.070
- "The Mitchells vs. the Machines"

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.440
is an animated movie about a teenage girl

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.040
who is leaving for college
for the first time,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.640
and her family, who's
trying to hold onto her,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.704
in the midst of a robot apocalypse
that threatens the world.

00:00:26.704 --> 00:00:28.031
(laser zapping)
- Get down!

00:00:28.031 --> 00:00:28.970
- Hi, my name is Mike Rianda.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.400
I'm the co-writer and director

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.380
of "The Mitchells vs. the Machines."

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.830
This is a scene in the
beginning of the movie,

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:35.517
and this is right after Rick and Katie

00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:38.680
had this big argument that
leads to their big falling out.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.540
Katie's excited to leave for
college and never look back,

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.490
and Rick sorta looks at the past

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.280
and how warm and sweet it was,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.530
and he's desperately
trying to get that back.

00:00:48.992 --> 00:00:51.742
(ethereal music)

00:00:53.208 --> 00:00:56.208
(objects clunking)

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:00.840
This scene was in the script.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.220
We were talking about trying to have

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.630
a visual symbol of their relationship,

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:07.030
and we were trying to sorta
figure out how to make it

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.020
more visual and tangible,
and then I remember,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.630
Jeff threw this kinda dusty
moose on a whiteboard.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:17.200
Right here is this dusty moose you drew.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.890
And then we thought that
was such a great symbol,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.130
because she hasn't thought about it,

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.340
and she even throws it away.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.450
And we were like, "Oh my
God, it'd be so brutal,

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.190
if you found something
that you gave to your kid

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.170
that meant a lot to you,
that they just threw away

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.590
when they left for college, so."

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.570
It was a way to try to
feel closer to Rick.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.600
We wanted the audience
to want what he wanted,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.277
which is like, "I've gotta
save this relationship."

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.330
We really wanted the scene to look

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.150
like it was a real teenage girl's room,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.630
which is a disaster. (laughs)

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.300
For her to have all of these strange,

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.730
sort of idiosyncratic things,

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.620
from like weird B movie
posters that she loves,

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.450
these little puppets that she makes.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.670
A part of this character is
just having her creativity

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.367
sort of exploding out of
the frame in every moment.

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.239
(melancholy music)

00:02:03.239 --> 00:02:05.090
(Katie sighs)

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.450
It was a real challenge
to get the hood on her.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.130
It's so funny, in 3D animation,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.810
the weirdest things
are hard and expensive.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.800
And they were like, "You
got one hoodie shot.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.490
Make it count," (laughs)
and we were like, "What?"

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.630
Two of the hardest things to do in 3D

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.740
are hair and cloth simulation.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.300
When cloth is interacting with hair,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.580
aw, it's a bonanza of money! (laughs)

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.530
So we tried to choose
our hoodie shot well,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.950
and this is an area where
we really wanted her

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.090
to feel bad and down,

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.524
so we chose this for the one moment.

00:02:38.524 --> 00:02:41.524
(melancholy music)

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.514
- Why is he like this?

00:02:44.514 --> 00:02:47.181
(water hissing)

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.770
- Why is she like this?

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.987
- Jeff wrote the lines,
"Why is he like this,"

00:02:52.987 --> 00:02:55.890
"Why is she like this," which
nicely sort of connected

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.230
the characters through editing.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.300
It was also, in that shot,
really important for her

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.990
to be looking at a
picture of her and her dad

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:01.870
when they got along well,

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.510
but you also wanna show the audience

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.610
that she too once valued
this relationship,

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.220
and she subtly would like it to return

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.850
to the way it used to be.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.260
It was really important
for the audience to know,

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.942
she does want a relationship with her dad.

00:03:15.942 --> 00:03:17.521
(water hissing)

00:03:17.521 --> 00:03:18.903
- Did you talk to her yet?

00:03:20.694 --> 00:03:23.500
- [Rick] (clears throat) No, not yet.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.760
- The performances in an
animated film are a handshake

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.340
between the voice actors
and the animators.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.010
The voice actors come first,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.880
and then the animators
animate to the voice track.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.200
And this scene is filled
with great performances.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.190
The animators' whole job
is making the characters

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:40.720
act like real people.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.030
Linda's, she's nervous,

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:43.770
and she doesn't wanna
confront her husband,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.810
but she also feels like
she needs to say something,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.360
so she's like rubbing her
hand and avoiding eye contact.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.440
Rick is washing dishes.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.293
There's a nice little finger tap there.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:55.330
One of the animators added that.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:56.940
It was so exciting because it meant

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.560
the movie was a little bit more alive,

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.690
and some artist was bringing
something new to it.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.620
These are digital puppets! (laughs)

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:05.220
They're not human beings.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.170
So any tool you can
use to try to make them

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.050
seem more real is like money in the bank.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.760
But seeing the animator
add something like that,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.613
it makes me so happy.

00:04:14.613 --> 00:04:16.390
- You just broke her laptop!

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.200
I mean, look, we haven't
had a good family picture

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.713
in years because you
two are always arguing!

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.820
- Huh, well, what about that one?

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.170
- That came with the frame!

00:04:27.123 --> 00:04:29.610
- This picture, we had in a screening,

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.030
and we loved this picture
and it made everyone laugh,

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:33.630
and then we couldn't get the rights to it.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.270
And then I hired this guy called The Wolf,

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.820
and he was able to track them down,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.790
and we got them to sign a
release and we were able

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:40.940
to use it in the movie!

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.003
- I know you can do this.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.380
- This is the first shot
we locked in the movie.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.270
And one of our artists, I
think it was Toby Wilson,

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:51.580
did this amazing color key that looked

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.710
exactly like we wanted
the whole movie to look.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.920
And then we worked with Mike Lasker

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.070
and his amazing team at Imageworks,

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:58.500
and Mike came from "Spider-Verse,"

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.430
so he knew the pipeline of 3D animation

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:02.020
to match a painting exactly.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.160
So this is the first shot
that we did for the movie,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.479
and I'm super proud of it.

00:05:07.479 --> 00:05:09.133
(melancholy music)

00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:11.200
This scene is really dark.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.130
It really sort of makes you
feel how devastated Rick is.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.310
We did this color key
and the entire studio

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.470
revolted against it, saying,
"This is too dark!" (laughs)

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.210
It has to be this dark
because Rick is in a bad place

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:24.840
emotionally and you have
to really feel that,

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.180
and also, when you watch
TV at night, it's dark.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.613
And I think it really does help the scene.

00:05:29.613 --> 00:05:31.300
(Katie and Rick laughing)

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.160
A lot of the reasons why
these shots are exteriors

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.800
is because Rick kind
of romanticizes nature.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.670
And the relationship he
had with his daughter

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.990
was that she did
everything he liked to do.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.720
So he liked to go into nature,
so she's going with him,

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.360
And so when he looks at the past,

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.777
he sees all those moments and he's like,

00:05:45.777 --> 00:05:47.460
"That's what we need to get back."

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.820
But what he really needs to
get back is sort of, you know,

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.800
seeing his daughter and
seeing who she's become,

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.211
and the movies that she makes,

00:05:54.211 --> 00:05:56.526
and the person that she is now.

00:05:56.526 --> 00:05:58.970
(Katie and Rick laughing)

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:00.700
We wanted this scene to be entirely

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:01.640
from Rick's point of view.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:02.860
This is his whole motive,

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.730
is to get back to these
days and seeing them

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.680
kind of through rose-colored
glasses a little bit.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.910
The shots are really overexposed,

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:11.710
'cause we were trying to get that feeling

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:13.300
of what home movies really look like,

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.093
and they often have bad lighting. (laughs)

00:06:17.093 --> 00:06:18.750
- Ba-ba-ba-ba-ba-ba-ba!
(Katie giggling)

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.170
- I love that piece of acting.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.350
That was pitched by Alan Hawkins,
who has a kid of his own,

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.140
and it's just like, (imitates munching)

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.840
but it's something I'd never
seen in an animated movie.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.710
It's something you would
never think to animate,

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.180
but all of the team brought

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.190
so many new ideas like this to the film,

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.964
and I think in the
storyboards, they just hugged.

00:06:34.964 --> 00:06:38.549
- And now, the Mitchell
Family Talent Show!

00:06:38.549 --> 00:06:40.099
♪ Mi-a-ha ha ♪

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:41.558
♪ Mi-a-hee ♪

00:06:41.558 --> 00:06:42.980
♪ Mi-a-hah ♪

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.790
- We literally added this
scene of them singing

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:48.050
to justify this act three payoff we had

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:49.820
with them singing together
at the end of the movie,

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:51.666
to try to make it feel cohesive.

00:06:51.666 --> 00:06:52.872
♪ Mi-a-huu ♪

00:06:52.872 --> 00:06:56.484
♪ Mi-a-ha ha ♪
(explosions booming)

00:06:56.484 --> 00:06:58.570
(Rick chuckling)
(sentimental music)

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:01.970
- Bye, Katie! Are you excited for camp?

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:04.040
- [Mike] This is the part
where you see that Rick

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.130
is actually a really good dad.
- Oh, hey-

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.010
- This is one of the most
important scenes in the movie,

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.460
and it took so long to tweak
the dialogue to be just right

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.560
and have Rick sort of give her this totem,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.140
this thing that's really important to him,

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.930
but also, he's being a goofball.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.711
Him trying to sort of do anything he could

00:07:20.711 --> 00:07:23.320
to cheer his daughter up
and get her to stop crying

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.100
and get her to feel happy and confident

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.830
about leaving for camp instead of scared.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:31.020
- You know how to say "I
love you" in moose language?

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.360
Let me just, ooh!
(Katie giggling)

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.930
Don't you laugh, come on,
you're supposed to be sad!

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:37.922
Hey, come on!

00:07:37.922 --> 00:07:39.450
(music fades)

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:43.096
- I love that shot of him sadly
looking at his reflection.

00:07:43.096 --> 00:07:46.096
(melancholy music)

00:07:53.326 --> 00:07:55.743
(Rick sighs)

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:00.053
- Alright, let's fix it.

00:08:00.937 --> 00:08:03.320
- And he stands up and
he says, "Let's fix it."

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.680
That's the action that basically inspires

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:06.133
the rest of the movie.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:08.480
I'm really proud of this scene.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.577
I've read so many people say,

00:08:10.577 --> 00:08:13.320
"I'm 14 minutes in and I'm
already crying," and every time,

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:14.930
I'm like, "Oh, it's this
scene, this is the scene!"

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:16.210
I check, 14 minutes in,

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:18.790
and I think it's really
a credit to the team,

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.760
sort of everyone firing on all cylinders.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.600
I think every single person
who touched it made better.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.070
It's easy for me to say,
because I'm just proud

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.850
of all the things that the
other artists did. (laughs)

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.010
They did all the good stuff,

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.879
and I just sort of was a
enthusiastic cheerleader for it.

00:08:34.879 --> 00:08:38.379
(gentle inspiring music)

